{"sequence": 16, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s1", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s1", "text": "(Modern Gaelic: \"Lughlagh mac Gille Chomghain\", known in English simply as Lulach, and nicknamed Tairbith, \"the Unfortunate\" and Fatuus, \"the Simple-minded\" or \"the Foolish\"; before 1033 – 17 March 1058) was King of Scots between 15 August 1057 and 17 March 1058.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 17, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s2", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s2", "text": "Lulach was the son of Gruoch of Scotland, from her first marriage to Gille Coemgáin, Mormaer of Moray, and thus the stepson of Macbeth (Mac Bethad mac Findlaích).", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 18, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s3", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s3", "text": "Following the death of Macbeth at the Battle of Lumphanan on 15 August 1057, the king's followers placed Lulach on the throne.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 19, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s4", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s4", "text": "He has the distinction of being the first king of Scotland of whom there are coronation details available: he was crowned, probably on 8 September 1057 at Scone.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 20, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s5", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s5", "text": "Lulach appears to have been a weak king, as his nicknames suggest, and ruled only for a few months before being assassinated and usurped by Malcolm III.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 21, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s6", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s6", "text": "Lulach's son Máel Snechtai was Mormaer of Moray, while Óengus of Moray was the son of Lulach's daughter.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
{"sequence": 22, "time_s": 6.391, "type": "evidence", "evidence": {"evidence_id": "d7s7", "title": "Lulach", "url": "https://trace.invalid/2wikimultihopqa_57df2e7e0bb011ebab90acde48001122_long/d7s7", "text": "He is believed to be buried on Saint Columba's Holy Island of Iona in or around the monastery.", "visible_time_s": 6.39100000000326, "requested_subject": "", "resolved_title": "Lulach", "entity_resolution": "unspecified"}}
